Show an out-of-range time as seconds in ls --full-time too.
ls --full-time falls back to seconds since the epoch when localtime()
returns NULL, but on Windows localtime() is a wrapper that substitutes
the epoch for a time the C runtime can't convert, one before 1970 or
after the year 3000. So such a file was listed with the time 1970-01-01
00:00:00 instead of its seconds, unlike the same file with plain -l.

Use localtime_or_null(), which returns NULL for such a time.

---
 coreutils/ls.c |    5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

--- a/coreutils/ls.c
+++ b/coreutils/ls.c
@@ -656,7 +656,12 @@
 			 * we don't show fractional seconds.
 			 */
 			char buf[sizeof("YYYY-mm-dd HH:MM:SS TIMEZONE")];
+#if ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
+			/* localtime() would give the epoch for an invalid time */
+			struct tm *tm = localtime_or_null(&dn->dn_time);
+#else
 			struct tm *tm = localtime(&dn->dn_time);
+#endif
 			if (tm)
 				strftime(buf, sizeof(buf), "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %z", tm);
 			else /* out of range: show seconds since the epoch */
